ion":"Woljeonggyo Bridge — photo 5","alt":"Woljeonggyo Bridge — gallery image 5"},{"id":27,"type":"photo","src":"/images/tours/woljeonggyo/woljeonggyo-cherry-reflection.webp","location":"Woljeonggyo Bridge","caption":"Woljeonggyo Bridge — photo 6","alt":"Woljeonggyo Bridge — gallery image 6"},{"id":28,"type":"photo","src":"/images/tours/daereungwon/chatgpt-image-2026-5-10-01-37-30.webp","location":"Daereungwon Tomb Complex & Hwangnidan-gil","alt":"Daereungwon Tomb Complex & Hwangnidan-gil — 1","caption":"Daereungwon Tomb Complex & Hwangnidan-gil — 1"},{"id":29,"type":"photo","src":"/images/tours/daereungwon/chatgpt-image-2026-5-10-01-37-33.webp","location":"Daereungwon Tomb Complex & Hwangnidan-gil","alt":"Daereungwon Tomb Complex & Hwangnidan-gil — 2","caption":"Daereungwon Tomb Complex & Hwangnidan-gil — 2"},{"id":30,"type":"photo","src":"/images/tours/daereungwon/daereungwon-cheonmachong-interior.webp","location":"Daereungwon Tomb Complex & Hwangnidan-gil","alt":"Daereungwon Tomb Complex & Hwangnidan-gil — 3","caption":"Daereungwon Tomb Complex & Hwangnidan-gil — 3"},{"id":31,"type":"photo","src":"/images/tours/gyochon-hanok-village/hwangnam-bread-flagship-1.webp","location":"Daereungwon Tomb Complex & Hwangnidan-gil","caption":"Gyochon Hanok Village — photo 12","alt":"Gyochon Hanok Village — gallery image 12"},{"id":32,"type":"photo","src":"/images/tours/gyochon-hanok-village/hwangnam-bread-flagship-2.webp","location":"Daereungwon Tomb Complex & Hwangnidan-gil","caption":"Gyochon Hanok Village — photo 13","alt":"Gyochon Hanok Village — gallery image 13"},{"id":33,"type":"photo","src":"/images/tours/gyeongju-national-museum/chatgpt-image-2026-5-11-12-21-49.webp","location":"Gyeongju National Museum","caption":"Gyeongju National Museum — photo 1","alt":"Gyeongju National Museum — gallery image 1"},{"id":34,"type":"photo","src":"/images/tours/gyeongju-national-museum/chatgpt-image-2026-5-11-12-21-56.webp","location":"Gyeongju National Museum","caption":"Gyeongju National Museum — photo 2","alt":"Gyeongju National Museum — gallery image 2"},{"id":35,"type":"photo","src":"/images/tours/gyeongju-national-museum/chatgpt-image-2026-5-11-12-31-57.webp","location":"Gyeongju National Museum","caption":"Gyeongju National Museum — photo 3","alt":"Gyeongju National Museum — gallery image 3"}],"itineraryStops":[{"number":1,"time":"08:10","duration":"30 min","name":"Busan subway pickup (3 stations)","category":"Pickup — three central Busan SUBWAY stations (not the railway station)","description":"Pickup runs in sequence at **three subway stations** in central Busan: **Busan Station Exit 4 (Subway Line 1) — 08:10**, **Seomyeon Station Exit 4 (Subway Lines 1 & 2) — 08:30**, **Haeundae Station Exit 5 (Subway Line 2) — 09:10**. \n\n**All three points are subway station exits.** At Busan Station in particular we meet you at **Exit 4 of the Line 1 subway station in front of the building — NOT inside the KTX railway terminal**: if you wait in the train station concourse you will not see the vehicle. Please remember the subway exit numbers exactly as written (Busan Station Exit 4 · Seomyeon Station Exit 4 · Haeundae Station Exit 5). \n\nPickup runs west→east, and after the final Haeundae stop we head north on the **Gyeongbu Expressway** toward Gyeongju, the ancient capital of **Silla (57 BCE – 935 CE)** — the longest-ruling dynasty in Korean history. The first destination, Ahopsan Bamboo Forest in Gijang-gun, is about 30 minutes from Haeundae.","highlights":["**All three points are subway station exits** — Busan Station Exit 4 · Seomyeon Station Exit 4 · Haeundae Station Exit 5","**Busan Station means Exit 4 of the Line 1 subway station, not the KTX railway terminal**","Busan Station 08:10 → Seomyeon Station 08:30 → Haeundae Station 09:10 (west→east)","Small-group mini-van (max ~14 pax)","About 30 minutes from the Haeundae pickup to Ahopsan Bamboo Forest","Printed bilingual (Korean/English) itinerary card distributed on boarding"],"timeUsed":["Busan Station Exit 4 (08:10) — 5 min","Seomyeon Station Exit 4 (08:30) — 5 min","Haeundae Station Exit 5 (09:10) — 5 min","Northbound expressway drive — ≈30 min"],"whyOnRoute":"The three pickup points cover Busan''s main hotel clusters (Busan Station/Choryang, mid-city Seomyeon, and the Haeundae beachfront) evenly across Subway Lines 1 and 2, so guests never have to cross the city early in the morning. The reason all three are stated as **subway exits** is simple: the Busan Station plaza mixes trains, buses and taxis and makes a vehicle hard to identify, while a subway exit number is something anyone finds in one go. Running west→east minimizes coach distance and lands the group at Ahopsan around 09:40 — early enough that the bamboo forest is at its quietest and most photogenic.","_poi_meta":{"poi_key":"OPS_busan_subway_pickup","kb_version":"1.16","verified_date":"2026-04-29"},"_role":"pickup"},{"number":2,"time":"≈ 09:40","duration":"60 min","name":"Ahopsan Bamboo Forest (아홉산숲)","category":"Private bamboo forest — K-drama filming site","description":"**Ahopsan Bamboo Forest** (''Nine Mountain Forest'', 아홉산숲) is a **520,000 m² private forest** in Gijang-gun, **managed by the Nampyeong Moon clan for ~400 years across 9 generations** — the family settled here after the **Imjin War (1592–1598)** and has continuously stewarded the land since. \n\nThe forest **opened to the public for the first time in 2016** and has rapidly become one of Korea''s most filmed natural locations. The signature path is the **Maengjongjuk bamboo corridor at Spot 9** — towering culms 15–20 m tall, swaying with the kind of dense whispering rustle that has earned the spot comparison to Kyoto''s Arashiyama. \n\n**Filming credits include**: **The King: Eternal Monarch (2020, SBS)**, **Moon Lovers: Scarlet Heart Ryeo (2016, SBS)**, **100 Days My Prince (2018, tvN)**, **Memories of the Sword (2015)**, and **Kundo: Age of the Rampant (2014)**. \n\nThe forest also protects **116 designated Korean pine trees over 400 years old** — Joseon-era plantings that survived the war and subsequent industrialization. \n\nAdmission: **Adult ₩8,000 / Child ₩5,000**. Hours: **09:00–18:00, closed Mondays**. The 75-minute dwell allows guests to walk the full numbered-spot loop (1→9) without rushing through the photogenic bamboo corridor. \n\nThe Nampyeong Moon clan continues to manage the site as a **sustainable working forest** rather than a pure tourist attraction — bamboo is still harvested for traditional Korean crafts including **zithers (gayageum bodies), baskets, and fishing rods**, and **commercial filming requires advance written permission**. \n\nBeyond Spot 9 the path winds into **Gutteo Grove and a second pine forest**, less photographed but equally serene.","highlights":["**520,000 m² private forest**, managed by Nampyeong Moon clan **400 years / 9 generations**","**Opened to the public 2016** — relatively new on the Jeju/Busan tour circuit","**Maengjongjuk bamboo corridor at Spot 9** — towering 15–20 m culms","**The King: Eternal Monarch (2020) + Moon Lovers (2016) + 100 Days My Prince (2018)** filming site","**116 protected Korean pine trees over 400 years old** (Joseon-era plantings)","Admission: Adult ₩8,000 / Child ₩5,000","Hours 09:00–18:00, **closed Mondays** (route avoids Mondays)","Calm-pace 75-min dwell lets guests walk the numbered-spot loop without rushing","**Sustainable working bamboo forest** — Nampyeong Moon clan still harvests bamboo for traditional Korean crafts (zithers, baskets, fishing rods); commercial filming requires advance written permission; **Spot 9 + Gutteo Grove + second pine forest** comprise the full numbered loop"],"timeUsed":["Coach park + entrance ticketing (~10 min)","Walk to Spot 9 Maengjongjuk bamboo corridor (~15 min)","Photo time + slow walk through bamboo (~25 min)","Return loop past 400-year-old Korean pines (~15 min)","Reassemble at coach (~10 min)"],"whyOnRoute":"Ahopsan opens the day with the calmest, most contemplative stop — a meditative bamboo walk before the more architectural Bulguksa midday segment. Selected over the more-famous Damyang Juknokwon (Jeolla province, 4+ hr from Busan) because Ahopsan is **35 min from Busan vs. 4 hr** while delivering a comparable bamboo-forest experience PLUS K-drama filming heritage. **The 9-spot loop pairs naturally with the calm-pace framing** of this tour — guests can take photos at every spot without feeling rushed.","_poi_meta":{"poi_key":"ahopsan_bamboo_forest","kb_version":"1.25","verified_date":"2026-04-29","sources":["Visit Korea","Wikipedia","Visit Busan official","Korea Tourism Organization (Ahopsan filming-location designation)","Cultural Heritage Administration of Korea (Nampyeong Moon clan working forest)"]},"smartNotes":{"photo":"Spot 9 (Maengjongjuk bamboo grove) — best 10:00–11:30 with side-light through the canopy; the thatched gate near the ancestral hall; 400-year pine corridor at the lower loop","facilities":"Small farm-stand at the entrance (seasonal local produce, simple drinks); no full café inside the forest","tip":"Wear darker tops for stronger photo contrast against the bright bamboo; do not step off the marked path (active forest restoration); drone use is prohibited"},"convenience":{"restroom":"Restrooms at the entrance gate and midway near the ancestral hall","parking":"On-site coach and car parking (free)"},"visitBasics":{"hours":"Daily 09:00–18:00 (last admission ~17:00)","closed":"Mondays","admission":"Adult ₩8,000 / Child ₩5,000","walking":"Moderate — gravel paths and gentle slopes; partial wooden walkway; comfortable shoes recommended"},"image":"/images/tours/ahopsan-bamboo/chatgpt-image-2026-5-10-12-22-47.webp","images":["/images/tours/ahopsan-bamboo/chatgpt-image-2026-5-10-12-22-47.webp","/images/tours/ahopsan-bamboo/chatgpt-image-2026-5-10-12-23-03.webp","/images/tours/ahopsan-bamboo/chatgpt-image-2026-5-10-12-23-07.webp","/images/tours/ahopsan-bamboo/ahopsan-entrance-marker.webp"],"imageCredits":[{"url":"/images/tours/ahopsan-bamboo/chatgpt-image-2026-5-10-12-22-47.webp","source":"atoc-korea"},{"url":"/images/tours/ahopsan-bamboo/chatgpt-image-2026-5-10-12-23-03.webp","source":"atoc-korea"},{"url":"/images/tours/ahopsan-bamboo/chatgpt-image-2026-5-10-12-23-07.webp","source":"atoc-korea"},{"url":"/images/tours/ahopsan-bamboo/ahopsan-entrance-marker.webp","source":"atoc-korea"}],"galleryItems":[{"id":1,"type":"photo","src":"/images/tours/ahopsan-bamboo/chatgpt-image-2026-5-10-12-22-47.webp","location":"Ahopsan Bamboo Forest (아홉산숲)","caption":"Ahopsan Bamboo Forest (아홉산숲) — photo 1","alt":"Ahopsan Bamboo Forest (아홉산숲) — gallery image 1"},{"id":2,"type":"photo","src":"/images/tours/ahopsan-bamboo/chatgpt-image-2026-5-10-12-23-03.webp","location":"Ahopsan Bamboo Forest (아홉산숲)","caption":"Ahopsan Bamboo Forest (아홉산숲) — photo 2","alt":"Ahopsan Bamboo Forest (아홉산숲) — gallery image 2"},{"id":3,"type":"photo","src":"/images/tours/ahopsan-bamboo/chatgpt-image-2026-5-10-12-23-07.webp","location":"Ahopsan Bamboo Forest (아홉산숲)","caption":"Ahopsan Bamboo Forest (아홉산숲) — photo 3","alt":"Ahopsan Bamboo Forest (아홉산숲) — gallery image 3"},{"id":4,"type":"photo","src":"/images/tours/ahopsan-bamboo/ahopsan-entrance-marker.webp","location":"Ahopsan Bamboo Forest (아홉산숲)","caption":"Ahopsan Bamboo Forest (아홉산숲) — photo 7","alt":"Ahopsan Bamboo Forest (아홉산숲) — gallery image 7"}]},{"number":3,"time":"≈ 11:30","duration":"75 min","name":"Bulguksa Temple (UNESCO World Heritage)","category":"UNESCO Buddhist temple — Silla masterpiece","description":"**Bulguksa Temple (불국사) — UNESCO World Heritage Site since 1995** (jointly with Seokguram Grotto) — is **the masterpiece of Unified Silla Buddhist art** and **Korea''s Historic and Scenic Site No. 1**. Originally founded in **528 CE under King Beopheung**, the current grand layout was commissioned in **751 CE by Prime Minister Kim Daeseong** (to honor his earthly parents) and completed in **774 CE** after his death. \n\nThe temple''s name means **''Temple of the Buddha Land''** — its stone-terrace layout maps Buddhist cosmology in three zones: **Daeungjeon (Hall of Great Enlightenment)** for the historical Buddha, **Geuknakjeon (Hall of Pure Land)** for Amitabha, and **Birojeon (Hall of Vairocana)** for the cosmic Buddha. \n\nThe wooden halls were destroyed by fire in the **1592–1598 Imjin War**; rebuilding spanned 1604–1805, and the **major modern restoration was 1969–1973 under President Park Chung-hee**. Bulguksa houses **six Korean National Treasures**: **Dabotap (NT 20, 10.4 m, image on Korea''s 10-won coin)**, **Seokgatap (NT 21, 8.2 m, inside which the world''s oldest extant woodblock-printed text — the Pure Light Dharani Sutra dated 704–751 CE — was discovered in 1966)**, the **Cheongungyo + Baegungyo bridges (NT 23, the symbolic 34-step path leading to Jahamun gate)**, the **Yeonhwagyo + Chilbogyo bridges (NT 22, leading to Geuknakjeon)**, and the **gilt-bronze Vairocana (NT 26)** + **gilt-bronze Maitreya (NT 27)**. \n\n**Admission has been FREE since 2023** under the revised Cultural Heritage Protection Act. Hours daily 09:00–18:00 (last admission 17:00), modest dress + remove shoes when entering halls + no drone photography. \n\nThe visit follows the temple''s pilgrimage axis: **Iljumun gate → Cheongungyo/Baegungyo (admire from below; foot traffic banned) → Jahamun → Daeungjeon courtyard with Dabotap and Seokgatap → Birojeon → Geuknakjeon**.","highlights":["**UNESCO World Heritage 1995** + **Korea''s Historic and Scenic Site No. 1**","Founded **528 CE → expanded 751 CE by PM Kim Daeseong → completed 774 CE**","**Six Korean National Treasures** in a single temple","**Dabotap (NT 20)** = image on Korea''s 10-won coin","**Seokgatap (NT 21) + Pure Light Dharani Sutra** = world''s oldest extant woodblock print (704–751 CE)","Cheongungyo/Baegungyo (NT 23): 34 steps = the 34 steps to Buddhist enlightenment","**FREE admission since 2023** (Cultural Heritage Protection Act revision)","Hours daily 09:00–18:00, last admission 17:00","**Imjin War 1593 destruction → 1604 partial rebuild → 1969–1973 comprehensive restoration** under President Park Chung-hee — heritage continuity narrative spanning four centuries of war, neglect, and modern conservation"],"timeUsed":["Coach drop-off + walk through pine-lined approach (~10 min)","Iljumun + Cheongungyo/Baegungyo photo time (~15 min)","Daeungjeon courtyard + Dabotap + Seokgatap (~25 min)","Birojeon Vairocana Hall + Geuknakjeon Pure Land Hall (~15 min)","Free time + reassemble at coach (~10 min)"],"whyOnRoute":"Bulguksa is **the essential UNESCO anchor of any Gyeongju day tour** and the most widely recognized Buddhist heritage site in Korea. The 11:30 arrival comes after the morning''s Korean school groups have cleared — they generally fill the courtyards between roughly 09:30 and 11:00 — so the temple grounds are noticeably quieter by the time you walk in. The 75 minutes allotted are enough to follow the full pilgrimage route without hurrying the **Dabotap and Seokgatap** pair. This is also **the last stop of the morning reached by van**: after lunch the van stops only twice, and the afternoon is spent on foot. And because Bulguksa''s stone steps and uneven ground are the most active stretch of the whole day, lunch follows immediately — so guests actually sit down.","_poi_meta":{"poi_key":"bulguksa_temple","kb_version":"1.25","verified_date":"2026-04-29","sources":["UNESCO World Heritage Centre","Wikipedia","World History Encyclopedia","VisitKorea","Visit Gyeongju official"]},"smartNotes":{"photo":"Cheongungyo / Baegungyo bridge stairway (NT 23) below Jahamun Gate; Dabotap (NT 20) and Seokgatap (NT 21) pagodas in the main courtyard; spring cherry blossoms along the approach road","facilities":"Tourist Information Center at the entrance; Bulguksa Museum (separate ticket 2,000 / 1,000 KRW, closed Mondays)","tip":"Modest dress; remove shoes when entering halls; no drones; do not photograph monks or worshippers (signs marked clearly); the temple is still an active Jogye Order head temple (11th district)"},"convenience":{"restroom":"Free public restrooms (mini-temple style architecture) inside the complex","parking":"On-site lot (paid)"},"visitBasics":{"hours":"Daily 09:00–18:00 (last admission 17:00)","closed":"Open year-round","admission":"Free (since 2023, per the revised Cultural Heritage Protection Act)","walking":"Moderate — stone steps, slopes and gravel paths; comfortable shoes recommended"},"image":"/images/tours/bulguksa-temple/chatgpt-image-2026-5-11-12-22-06.webp","images":["/images/tours/bulguksa-temple/chatgpt-image-2026-5-11-12-22-06.webp","/images/tours/bulguksa-temple/chatgpt-image-2026-5-11-12-22-17.webp","/images/tours/bulguksa-temple/chatgpt-image-2026-5-11-12-22-30.webp","/images/tours/bulguksa-temple/photo-001.webp","/images/tours/bulguksa-temple/photo-002.webp","/images/tours/bulguksa-temple/bulguksa-dabotap-courtyard.webp"],"imageCredits":[{"url":"/images/tours/bulguksa-temple/chatgpt-image-2026-5-11-12-22-06.webp","source":"atoc-korea"},{"url":"/images/tours/bulguksa-temple/chatgpt-image-2026-5-11-12-22-17.webp","source":"atoc-korea"},{"url":"/images/tours/bulguksa-temple/chatgpt-image-2026-5-11-12-22-30.webp","source":"atoc-korea"},{"url":"/images/tours/bulguksa-temple/photo-001.webp","source":"atoc-korea"},{"url":"/images/tours/bulguksa-temple/photo-002.webp","source":"atoc-korea"},{"url":"/images/tours/bulguksa-temple/chatgpt-image-2026-5-11-12-22-06.webp","source":"atoc-korea"},{"url":"/images/tours/bulguksa-temple/bulguksa-dabotap-courtyard.webp","source":"atoc-korea"},{"url":"/images/tours/bulguksa-temple/photo-001.webp","source":"atoc-korea"},{"url":"/images/tours/bulguksa-temple/photo-002.webp","source":"atoc-korea"}],"galleryItems":[{"id":1,"type":"photo","src":"/images/tours/bulguksa-temple/chatgpt-image-2026-5-11-12-22-06.webp","location":"Bulguksa Temple (UNESCO World Heritage)","caption":"Bulguksa Temple (UNESCO World Heritage) — photo 1","alt":"Bulguksa Temple (UNESCO World Heritage) — gallery image 1"},{"id":2,"type":"photo","src":"/images/tours/bulguksa-temple/chatgpt-image-2026-5-11-12-22-17.webp","location":"Bulguksa Temple (UNESCO World Heritage)","caption":"Bulguksa Temple (UNESCO World Heritage) — photo 2","alt":"Bulguksa Temple (UNESCO World Heritage) — gallery image 2"},{"id":3,"type":"photo","src":"/images/tours/bulguksa-temple/chatgpt-image-2026-5-11-12-22-30.webp","location":"Bulguksa Temple (UNESCO World Heritage)","caption":"Bulguksa Temple (UNESCO World Heritage) — photo 3","alt":"Bulguksa Temple (UNESCO World Heritage) — gallery image 3"},{"id":4,"type":"photo","src":"/images/tours/bulguksa-temple/photo-001.webp","location":"Bulguksa Temple (UNESCO World Heritage)","caption":"Bulguksa Temple (UNESCO World Heritage) — photo 4","alt":"Bulguksa Temple (UNESCO World Heritage) — gallery image 4"},{"id":5,"type":"photo","src":"/images/tours/bulguksa-temple/photo-002.webp","location":"Bulguksa Temple (UNESCO World Heritage)","caption":"Bulguksa Temple (UNESCO World Heritage) — photo 5","alt":"Bulguksa Temple (UNESCO World Heritage) — gallery image 5"},{"id":6,"type":"photo","src":"/images/tours/bulguksa-temple/bulguksa-dabotap-courtyard.webp","location":"Bulguksa Temple (UNESCO World Heritage)","caption":"Bulguksa Temple (UNESCO World Heritage) — photo 7","alt":"Bulguksa Temple (UNESCO World Heritage) — gallery image 7"}]},{"number":4,"time":"≈ 13:00","duration":"60 min","name":"Lunch (Gyeongju local restaurant)","category":"Meal — Gyeongju regional cuisine","description":"Lunch comes as the van moves from Bulguksa into central Gyeongju, at a local restaurant picked by the guide. The signature dish is **Gyeongju-style ssambap** (rice with seasonal banchan and lettuce wraps — a local meal style that became widely known in the 1970s, reflecting the region''s agricultural abundance). \n\n**Vegetarian and allergy requests are arranged if you flag them the day before.** The meal is not included in the tour price and runs **about ₩15,000–₩25,000 per person**. The 60 minutes allotted let you eat and use the restrooms without watching the clock — **because the afternoon is spent walking two blocks**, resting properly here is what sets the pace for the rest of the day.","highlights":["Guide-picked local restaurant in central Gyeongju","**Gyeongju-style ssambap** — rice with seasonal banchan and lettuce wraps","**Vegetarian and allergy requests** arranged if flagged the day before","**60 minutes allotted** — the proper rest before the afternoon''s two walked blocks","Cost about **₩15,000–₩25,000** per person, NOT included in the tour price"],"timeUsed":["Walk from Bulguksa exit to restaurant (~5 min)","Order + meal (~50 min)","Reassemble at coach (~5 min)"],"whyOnRoute":"This is where you sit down after Bulguksa''s steps and uneven stone, back in central Gyeongju. The afternoon is **covered on foot in two blocks** (Gyochon and the Choi Family House → Woljeonggyo, then Daereungwon → Hwangnidan-gil), so resting properly here is what sets the pace for the rest of the day. The restaurant serves **ssambap**, the local Gyeongju style, and vegetarian or allergy requests are arranged if you flag them the day before. The 55–60 minutes allotted mean nobody has to hurry — restrooms included.","_poi_meta":{"poi_key":"OPS_gyeongju_lunch","kb_version":"1.16","verified_date":"2026-04-29"}},{"number":5,"time":"≈ 14:10","duration":"75 min","name":"Gyochon Hanok Village & Choi Family House & Woljeonggyo Bridge","category":"Joseon head-family hanok village + reconstructed Silla wooden bridge (one drop-off, walked)","description":"**Gyochon Hanok Village** and **Woljeonggyo Bridge** face each other across the Namcheon River, linked by a **10–15 minute riverside walk**, so this tour makes **one single drop-off and walks both**. \n\nThe heart of **Gyochon** is the **Choi Family House — the head house of the Gyeongju Choi clan**, a merchant-aristocrat family that **held its wealth across 12 generations and 300 years** in the late Joseon era, and is designated **Important Folklore Material No. 27**. The family''s six house rules (**never amass wealth beyond 10,000 sok**, **never buy up another''s fields in a famine year**, **let no one within a hundred li starve**, and three more) are Korea''s best-known example of noblesse oblige. The clan''s liquor, **Gyodong Beopju, is Important Intangible Cultural Asset No. 86-3**, brewed inside the head house to a **350-year-old recipe** and sold on site (about ₩30,000 for a 750 ml bottle as of 2026). Right beside the village stand **Gyeongjuhyanggyo** (the Joseon-era regional Confucian academy) and the **site of Yoseokgung** — the residence of 7th-century Silla Princess Yoseok, daughter of King Muyeol and **mother of Seol Chong**, the monk-scholar who codified the Idu writing system. \n\nAcross the river, **Woljeonggyo is Korea''s largest fully wooden bridge**. The **Samguk Sagi** records its construction in **760 CE, under King Gyeongdeok, the 35th ruler of Silla**. Lost during the Joseon Dynasty, it was **excavated in 1984–1986, its main span reconstructed in 2008–2013, and two gatehouses added in 2016–2018**, reopening in **April 2018** — **1,258 years after it was first built**. The span measures **60.57 m long and 13 m wide**, and the reconstruction used **traditional Korean hanok timber joinery with no modern metal fasteners**. It was **designated a Historic Site in 2004** and named one of the **''100 Best Tourist Attractions in Korea 2025–2026''** by the Ministry of Culture, Sports and Tourism. \n\n**Both places are free to enter** (a few individual houses inside the village charge their own ₩2,000–3,000). The village fills with **cherry blossoms in early April** and **golden ginkgos in late October**.","highlights":["**Two places, one drop-off** — Gyochon ↔ Woljeonggyo is a 10–15 min walk along the Namcheon","**Choi Family House = head house of the Gyeongju Choi clan, Important Folklore Material No. 27** — 12 generations, 300 years, six house rules","**Gyodong Beopju = Important Intangible Cultural Asset No. 86-3** — 350-year recipe, brewed and sold inside the head house","**Site of Yoseokgung** — 7th-century home of Princess Yoseok, mother of Seol Chong (Idu script)","**Woljeonggyo = Korea''s largest wooden bridge** (60.57 m × 13 m), built **760 CE** per the Samguk Sagi","**Reopened April 2018 — 1,258 years after the original**, designated a Historic Site in 2004","**Zero modern metal fasteners** in the reconstruction — traditional hanok timber joinery","**''100 Best Tourist Attractions in Korea 2025–2026''** (Ministry of Culture, Sports and Tourism)","**Both free to enter** · the village is flat, the bridge a gentle slope"],"timeUsed":["Choi Family House courtyard + main lane walk (≈20 min)","Gyodong Beopju tasting + souvenir purchase (≈10 min)","Riverside walk along the Namcheon (≈10 min)","Crossing Woljeonggyo + gatehouse interpretive exhibit + photos (≈25 min)","River panorama + reassemble (≈10 min)"],"whyOnRoute":"After lunch this is where the day shifts **from the grand royal-Buddhist scale of Bulguksa to the scale of lived history** — Gyeongju seen through a merchant family''s eyes rather than a king''s. The two places are bundled into one stop because the geography already does it: the southern edge of Gyochon **is** the north bank of Woljeonggyo, so **there is no reason to park twice**, and the time saved on boarding becomes time on foot. The 75-minute block lets you do both the Beopju tasting and the photos on the bridge without hurrying. Mid-afternoon is also when Woljeonggyo shows its **timber detail and river reflections most clearly in daylight**, and you avoid the crowd that gathers at sunset.","_poi_meta":{"poi_key":"gyochon_hanok_village","kb_version":"1.25","verified_date":"2026-08-04","sources":["Visit Gyeongju official","Cultural Heritage Administration","Wikipedia (Choi Clan, Yoseokgung)","Korea Tourism Organization (Gyeongju Choi Clan + Gyochon Hanok Village)","Cultural Heritage Administration (Important Folklore Material No. 27 designation)"],"is_compound_stop":true,"secondary_poi_keys":["woljeonggyo_bridge"],"compound_stop_note":"Gyochon Hanok Village (incl. the Choi Family House, Important Folklore Material No. 27) and Woljeonggyo Bridge sit on opposite banks of the Namcheon, 10–15 min apart on foot, and are visited as ONE 75-min drop-off. Both remain separate POIs in the KB."},"smartNotes":{"photo":"The inner courtyard and wall lines of the Choi Family House; Woljeonggyo seen whole from the Namcheon bank, with its reflection on the water; cherry blossoms in early April, ginkgos in late October","facilities":"Gyodong Beopju shop (souvenir bottles); small artisan stores along the main lane (traditional rice cake, hwangnam-bbang); Yosukgung 1779 (luxury restaurant, reservation required)","tip":"Gyodong Beopju is sold directly inside the head house (750 ml, about ₩30,000, boxed on request). The Woljeonggyo gatehouse holds an interpretive exhibit on the reconstruction archaeology — five minutes is enough to take it in."},"convenience":{"restroom":"Public restrooms at the village entrance and near Gyeongjuhyanggyo","parking":"Coach drop-off lot at the western entrance (free)"},"visitBasics":{"hours":"Gyochon village open at all times (most heritage houses 09:00–18:00) · Woljeonggyo open 24 hours","closed":"Some individual houses close on Mondays — the main lanes and Woljeonggyo are always open","admission":"Both free (a few houses inside the village charge their own ₩2,000–3,000)","walking":"Easy — gravel and stone-flag lanes in the village; a flat 10–15 min riverside walk along the Namcheon to the bridge"},"image":"/images/tours/gyochon-hanok-village/chatgpt-image-2026-5-11-12-33-48.webp","images":["/images/tours/gyochon-hanok-village/chatgpt-image-2026-5-11-12-33-48.webp","/images/tours/gyochon-hanok-village/chatgpt-image-2026-5-11-12-33-53.webp","/images/tours/gyochon-hanok-village/chatgpt-image-2026-5-11-12-33-57.webp","/images/tours/gyochon-hanok-village/chatgpt-image-2026-5-11-12-34-35.webp","/images/tours/gyochon-hanok-village/photo-001.webp","/images/tours/gyochon-hanok-village/photo-002.webp","/images/tours/gyochon-hanok-village/photo-003.webp","/images/tours/gyochon-hanok-village/photo-004.webp","/images/tours/gyochon-hanok-village/photo-005.webp","/images/tours/gyochon-hanok-village/photo-006.webp","/images/tours/gyochon-hanok-village/gyochon-hanok-village-marker.webp","/images/tours/gyochon-hanok-village/hwangnam-bread-flagship-1.webp","/images/tours/gyochon-hanok-village/hwangnam-bread-flagship-2.webp"],"imageCredits":[{"url":"/images/tours/gyochon-hanok-village/chatgpt-image-2026-5-11-12-33-48.webp","source":"atoc-korea"},{"url":"/images/tours/gyochon-hanok-village/chatgpt-image-2026-5-11-12-33-53.webp","source":"atoc-korea"},{"url":"/images/tours/gyochon-hanok-village/chatgpt-image-2026-5-11-12-33-57.webp","source":"atoc-korea"},{"url":"/images/tours/gyochon-hanok-village/chatgpt-image-2026-5-11-12-34-35.webp","source":"atoc-korea"},{"url":"/images/tours/gyochon-hanok-village/photo-001.webp","source":"atoc-korea"},{"url":"/images/tours/gyochon-hanok-village/photo-002.webp","source":"atoc-korea"},{"url":"/images/tours/gyochon-hanok-village/photo-003.webp","source":"atoc-korea"},{"url":"/images/tours/gyochon-hanok-village/photo-004.webp","source":"atoc-korea"},{"url":"/images/tours/gyochon-hanok-village/photo-005.webp","source":"atoc-korea"},{"url":"/images/tours/gyochon-hanok-village/photo-006.webp","source":"atoc-korea"},{"url":"/images/tours/gyochon-hanok-village/gyochon-hanok-village-marker.webp","source":"atoc-korea"},{"url":"/images/tours/gyochon-hanok-village/hwangnam-bread-flagship-1.webp","source":"atoc-korea"},{"url":"/images/tours/gyochon-hanok-village/hwangnam-bread-flagship-2.webp","source":"atoc-korea"}],"galleryItems":[{"id":1,"type":"photo","src":"/images/tours/gyochon-hanok-village/chatgpt-image-2026-5-11-12-33-48.webp","location":"Gyochon Hanok Village","caption":"Gyochon Hanok Village — photo 1","alt":"Gyochon Hanok Village — gallery image 1"},{"id":2,"type":"photo","src":"/images/tours/gyochon-hanok-village/chatgpt-image-2026-5-11-12-33-53.webp","location":"Gyochon Hanok Village","caption":"Gyochon Hanok Village — photo 2","alt":"Gyochon Hanok Village — gallery image 2"},{"id":3,"type":"photo","src":"/images/tours/gyochon-hanok-village/chatgpt-image-2026-5-11-12-33-57.webp","location":"Gyochon Hanok Village","caption":"Gyochon Hanok Village — photo 3","alt":"Gyochon Hanok Village — gallery image 3"},{"id":4,"type":"photo","src":"/images/tours/gyochon-hanok-village/chatgpt-image-2026-5-11-12-34-35.webp","location":"Gyochon Hanok Village","caption":"Gyochon Hanok Village — photo 4","alt":"Gyochon Hanok Village — gallery image 4"},{"id":5,"type":"photo","src":"/images/tours/gyochon-hanok-village/photo-001.webp","location":"Gyochon Hanok Village","caption":"Gyochon Hanok Village — photo 5","alt":"Gyochon Hanok Village — gallery image 5"},{"id":6,"type":"photo","src":"/images/tours/gyochon-hanok-village/photo-002.webp","location":"Gyochon Hanok Village","caption":"Gyochon Hanok Village — photo 6","alt":"Gyochon Hanok Village — gallery image 6"},{"id":7,"type":"photo","src":"/images/tours/gyochon-hanok-village/photo-003.webp","location":"Gyochon Hanok Village","caption":"Gyochon Hanok Village — photo 7","alt":"Gyochon Hanok Village — gallery image 7"},{"id":8,"type":"photo","src":"/images/tours/gyochon-hanok-village/photo-004.webp","location":"Gyochon Hanok Village","caption":"Gyochon Hanok Village — photo 8","alt":"Gyochon Hanok Village — gallery image 8"},{"id":9,"type":"photo","src":"/images/tours/gyochon-hanok-village/photo-005.webp","location":"Gyochon Hanok Village","caption":"Gyochon Hanok Village — photo 9","alt":"Gyochon Hanok Village — gallery image 9"},{"id":10,"type":"photo","src":"/images/tours/gyochon-hanok-village/photo-006.webp","location":"Gyochon Hanok Village","caption":"Gyochon Hanok Village — photo 10","alt":"Gyochon Hanok Village — gallery image 10"},{"id":11,"type":"photo","src":"/images/tours/gyochon-hanok-village/gyochon-hanok-village-marker.webp","location":"Gyochon Hanok Village","caption":"Gyochon Hanok Village — photo 11","alt":"Gyochon Hanok Village — gallery image 11"},{"id":1,"type":"photo","src":"/images/itinerary/woljeonggyo-front-day.webp","location":"Woljeonggyo Bridge","caption":"Woljeonggyo Bridge — photo 1","alt":"Woljeonggyo Bridge — gallery image 1"},{"id":2,"type":"photo","src":"/images/itinerary/woljeonggyo-reflection.webp","location":"Woljeonggyo Bridge","caption":"Woljeonggyo Bridge — photo 2","alt":"Woljeonggyo Bridge — gallery image 2"},{"id":3,"type":"photo","src":"/images/itinerary/woljeonggyo-pavilion-night.webp","location":"Woljeonggyo Bridge","caption":"Woljeonggyo Bridge — photo 3","alt":"Woljeonggyo Bridge — gallery image 3"},{"id":4,"type":"photo","src":"/images/tours/woljeonggyo/photo-001.webp","location":"Woljeonggyo Bridge","caption":"Woljeonggyo Bridge — photo 4","alt":"Woljeonggyo Bridge — gallery image 4"},{"id":5,"type":"photo","src":"/images/tours/woljeonggyo/photo-002.webp","location":"Woljeonggyo Bridge","caption":"Woljeonggyo Bridge — photo 5","alt":"Woljeonggyo Bridge — gallery image 5"},{"id":6,"type":"photo","src":"/images/tours/woljeonggyo/woljeonggyo-cherry-reflection.webp","location":"Woljeonggyo Bridge","caption":"Woljeonggyo Bridge — photo 6","alt":"Woljeonggyo Bridge — gallery image 6"}]},{"number":6,"time":"≈ 15:35","duration":"75 min","name":"Daereungwon Tomb Complex & Hwangnidan-gil","category":"Silla royal tomb park + hanok cafe street (walked, one drop-off)","description":"**Daereungwon** is a tomb park of **126,500 m²** of gentle grass mounds holding **23 tombs of Silla kings, queens and nobles**, most of them raised in the **4th–6th centuries**. The most famous is **Cheonmachong (Tomb 155)** — **excavated on April 16, 1973**, 12.7 m high and about 47 m across, and **the only tomb in Daereungwon whose interior is open to visitors**. The dig produced **11,526 artifacts**, among them the **Cheonmachong Gold Crown (National Treasure No. 188)** and the **Cheonmado — the only Silla-era painting that survives**, drawn on a birch-bark saddle flap (the crown itself is on display later in the day, at Gyeongju National Museum). Neighbouring **Hwangnamdaechong** is the largest tomb in the complex: its twin north and south mounds hold **an unidentified Silla king and queen buried together**, and the queen''s gold crown carries **3 pairs** of pendants where a king''s normally carries one — a hint at how high Silla''s early royal women stood. **Park admission is free** (Cheonmachong''s interior costs adult ₩3,000 / youth ₩2,000 / child ₩1,000). Climbing the mounds is prohibited. \n\n**Directly across Daereungwon''s west wall** is **Hwangnidan-gil**. The name fuses **Hwangnam-dong with Gyeongnidan-gil** (Seoul''s Itaewon street) — it literally means **''Hwangnam-dong''s Gyeongnidan-gil''**. Running **from Naenam Intersection to the Hwangnam Elementary School intersection**, both sides are lined with **cafes, restaurants, studios and gift shops converted out of hanok houses**, and **since around 2017** it has been the youngest street in Gyeongju. This neighbourhood is also the home of **Hwangnam bread** — the thin wheat pastry filled with red-bean paste **first made in 1939 in Hwangnam-dong by Choi Yeong-hwa**, and the souvenir travellers carry out of Gyeongju more than any other. \n\nBecause tombs and cafe street are separated by **a single wall**, this tour walks **1,500 years and the 2020s side by side on one drop-off**.","highlights":["**Daereungwon = 23 Silla royal tombs across 126,500 m²** (4th–6th centuries)","**Cheonmachong (Tomb 155)** — excavated April 16, 1973, **the only interior open to visitors**, **11,526 artifacts**","**Cheonmado = the only surviving Silla-era painting** · Cheonmachong Gold Crown = **National Treasure No. 188**","**Hwangnamdaechong** — the complex''s largest, a king and queen buried together. Queen''s crown pendants **3 pairs** > the king''s one","**Free park admission** (Cheonmachong interior adult ₩3,000) · flat grass paths, easy for strollers and older guests","**Hwangnidan-gil = ''Hwangnam-dong''s Gyeongnidan-gil''** — Hwangnam-dong + Gyeongnidan-gil","**Straight across Daereungwon''s west wall** — Naenam Intersection to Hwangnam Elementary School intersection","A street of **hanok-converted cafes, studios and gift shops**, Gyeongju''s youngest since around 2017","**Hwangnam bread = first made in 1939 in Hwangnam-dong by Choi Yeong-hwa** — this is the neighbourhood it came from"],"timeUsed":["Enter at the Daereungwon main gate + walk among the mounds (≈25 min)","Cheonmachong interior (optional, ≈15 min)","Out through the west gate → into Hwangnidan-gil (≈5 min)","Free time in the Hwangnidan-gil cafes and shops (≈20 min)","Hwangnam bread and other souvenirs + reassemble (≈10 min)"],"whyOnRoute":"After the Joseon-and-Silla heritage block of Gyochon and Woljeonggyo, this is **the one stop of the day in the present tense**. Daereungwon has no stairs and no climb — flat grass paths make it the easiest walking of the day — and one wall further on there is coffee and red-bean bread on Hwangnidan-gil. The fact that **a royal tomb and a cafe are a one-minute walk apart** is itself the character of this city. Late afternoon is when Hwangnidan-gil is at its liveliest, and it lets guests handle **restrooms, coffee and souvenirs in one place** before the final stop (the museum, or Donggung & Wolji). The **actual** gold crown is at Gyeongju National Museum, the next stop — so seeing the excavation site at Cheonmachong and then the artifact at the museum makes **the order itself the story**.","image":"/images/tours/daereungwon/chatgpt-image-2026-5-10-01-37-30.webp","images":["/images/tours/daereungwon/chatgpt-image-2026-5-10-01-37-30.webp","/images/tours/daereungwon/chatgpt-image-2026-5-10-01-37-33.webp","/images/tours/daereungwon/daereungwon-cheonmachong-interior.webp"],"imageCredits":[{"url":"/images/tours/daereungwon/chatgpt-image-2026-5-10-01-37-30.webp","source":"atoc-korea"},{"url":"/images/tours/daereungwon/chatgpt-image-2026-5-10-01-37-33.webp","source":"atoc-korea"},{"url":"/images/tours/daereungwon/daereungwon-cheonmachong-interior.webp","source":"atoc-korea"}],"galleryItems":[{"id":0,"type":"photo","src":"/images/tours/daereungwon/chatgpt-image-2026-5-10-01-37-30.webp","location":"Daereungwon Tomb Complex & Hwangnidan-gil","alt":"Daereungwon Tomb Complex & Hwangnidan-gil — 1","caption":"Daereungwon Tomb Complex & Hwangnidan-gil — 1"},{"id":0,"type":"photo","src":"/images/tours/daereungwon/chatgpt-image-2026-5-10-01-37-33.webp","location":"Daereungwon Tomb Complex & Hwangnidan-gil","alt":"Daereungwon Tomb Complex & Hwangnidan-gil — 2","caption":"Daereungwon Tomb Complex & Hwangnidan-gil — 2"},{"id":0,"type":"photo","src":"/images/tours/daereungwon/daereungwon-cheonmachong-interior.webp","location":"Daereungwon Tomb Complex & Hwangnidan-gil","alt":"Daereungwon Tomb Complex & Hwangnidan-gil — 3","caption":"Daereungwon Tomb Complex & Hwangnidan-gil — 3"},{"id":12,"type":"photo","src":"/images/tours/gyochon-hanok-village/hwangnam-bread-flagship-1.webp","location":"Daereungwon Tomb Complex & Hwangnidan-gil","caption":"Gyochon Hanok Village — photo 12","alt":"Gyochon Hanok Village — gallery image 12"},{"id":13,"type":"photo","src":"/images/tours/gyochon-hanok-village/hwangnam-bread-flagship-2.webp","location":"Daereungwon Tomb Complex & Hwangnidan-gil","caption":"Gyochon Hanok Village — photo 13","alt":"Gyochon Hanok Village — gallery image 13"}],"visitBasics":{"hours":"Daereungwon 09:00–22:00 (to 21:00 in winter) · Hwangnidan-gil shops generally 10:00–21:00 (varies by shop)","closed":"Open year-round","admission":"Daereungwon park free · Cheonmachong interior adult ₩3,000 / youth ₩2,000 / child ₩1,000 · Hwangnidan-gil itself free","walking":"Easy — flat grass paths between the mounds (126,500 m²), comfortable for strollers and older guests. Hwangnidan-gil is a flat pavement"},"convenience":{"restroom":"Public restrooms at the Daereungwon main gate and near Cheonmachong · in-store restrooms when you use a Hwangnidan-gil cafe","parking":"Paid car park at the Daereungwon main gate"},"smartNotes":{"photo":"Daereungwon''s stone-wall lane (cherry tunnel, early April) · the magnolias behind Hwangnamdaechong · late-afternoon sun setting between the mounds · the hanok cafe lanes of Hwangnidan-gil","facilities":"A small exhibit inside Cheonmachong (Cheonmado replica and grave goods) · combined Daereungwon + Donggung & Wolji day pass on sale (₩4,000–8,000) · plenty of cafes and gift shops on Hwangnidan-gil","tip":"Climbing the mounds is prohibited (fines apply). The Daereungwon walk takes 30–60 minutes, and the west gate opens straight onto Hwangnidan-gil. The original Hwangnam bread shop is in this neighbourhood and the queue is long on weekend afternoons."},"_poi_meta":{"poi_key":"daereungwon_tomb_complex","kb_version":"1.29","verified_date":"2026-08-04","is_compound_stop":true,"secondary_poi_keys":["hwangnidan_gil"],"compound_stop_note":"Daereungwon (23 Silla royal tombs incl. Cheonmachong, 11,526 artifacts) and Hwangnidan-gil (the hanok cafe street literally named ''Hwangnam-dong''s Gyeongnidan-gil'') are separated only by Daereungwon''s west wall and are visited as ONE 75-min drop-off. hwangnidan_gil is not yet a POI KB entry — facts here verified 2026-08-04 (namu.wiki, VisitKorea, Gyeongju city map).","sources":["POI KB v1.29 (daereungwon_tomb_complex)","VisitKorea (Hwangnidan-gil)","Gyeongju city tourism map","Wikipedia (Cheonmachong / Hwangnamdaechong)"]}},{"number":7,"time":"≈ 17:00","duration":"60 min","name":"Gyeongju National Museum (in winter, Donggung & Wolji by night)","category":"National museum of Silla artifacts — replaced Nov–Feb by the Donggung & Wolji night view","description":"The last stop of the day is one of two, depending on the season. \n\n**① Mar–Oct — Gyeongju National Museum (default).** **Korea''s premier museum of Silla artifacts**, founded as a branch museum in **1945**, with the main building opened in **1968** and **National Museum status granted in 1975**. The collection runs to **over 100,000 items, including more than 30,000 designated Cultural Heritage pieces**. The core exhibits are **(1) the Cheonmachong Gold Crown (National Treasure No. 188)** — the very object taken from the tomb you saw at Daereungwon, unearthed in 1973 — and **(2) the Sacred Bell of King Seongdeok, the Emille Bell (National Treasure No. 29)** — cast in **771 CE**, about 19 tons, one of the largest bronze bells still in existence (the legend that a child was cast into it was laid to rest as folklore when **alloy analysis in 1998 found no human remains**). The **Wolji Hall** displays **more than 33,000 artifacts recovered when Wolji (Anapji) was dredged in 1975–1976** — wooden objects, roof tiles and the famous drinking-game die, preserved in oxygen-starved mud — which pairs it directly with Donggung & Wolji next door. **Hours 10:00–18:00 (last admission 17:30); regular closures are Jan 1, Seollal and Chuseok only, and it is open as normal on Mondays. Permanent exhibitions are free.** From March to October it stays open until 20:00 on Saturdays. \n\n**② Nov–Feb — Donggung & Wolji (winter night view).** In winter the sun is already low by about 17:00, so at the same hour we go instead to **Gyeongju''s finest night view**. **Donggung & Wolji (formerly Anapji, Historic Site No. 18)** is the **detached palace and pond of the Silla royal court, created in 674 by King Munmu**, famous for its pavilions mirrored on the water — and the 33,000-plus artifacts in the Wolji Hall above came from the bed of this very pond. **09:00–22:00 (ticketing closes 21:30), open every day of the year. Admission adult ₩3,000 / youth and soldiers ₩2,000 / child ₩1,000.** Winter sunset falls at around **17:15–17:30**, so this tour''s arrival time (about 17:00) puts you there **from dusk through to the moment the lights come on**. \n\nThe two sites are **5 minutes apart by car**, and your guide will confirm which one applies on the day, based on the season, the sunset time and conditions on the ground.","highlights":["**Mar–Oct = Gyeongju National Museum** / **Nov–Feb = Donggung & Wolji by night** — a last stop the season decides","**Cheonmachong Gold Crown (National Treasure No. 188)** — the actual crown from the tomb you saw at the previous stop","**Emille Bell / Sacred Bell of King Seongdeok (National Treasure No. 29)** — cast 771 CE, ~19 tons, among the largest bronze bells extant","**Wolji Hall = 33,000+ artifacts from the 1975–1976 dredging of Wolji** — the twin of Donggung & Wolji","Museum **10:00–18:00 (last admission 17:30) · permanent exhibitions free**","**Open as normal on Mondays** — regular closures are Jan 1, Seollal and Chuseok only","**Donggung & Wolji = Historic Site No. 18**, the Silla detached palace and pond created in 674 by King Munmu (formerly Anapji)","Donggung & Wolji **09:00–22:00 (ticketing closes 21:30) · open year-round · adult ₩3,000**","Winter sunset **17:15–17:30** — arriving about 17:00 means you watch it from dusk to illumination"],"timeUsed":["Drop-off + entry (≈5 min)","Silla History Hall + Silla Art Hall (≈20 min) / winter: one loop of the pond (≈25 min)","Wolji Hall + the outdoor Emille Bell (≈20 min) / winter: night-view photography (≈20 min)","Cheonmachong Gold Crown room (≈10 min) / winter: waiting for the lights to come on (≈10 min)","Free time + restroom + reassemble (≈5 min)"],"whyOnRoute":"Seeing the **actual gold crown** immediately after standing at the excavation site in Daereungwon closes the day as a single sentence: **tomb → artifact**. Putting it last comes out of the arithmetic — an arrival around 17:00 is **30 minutes ahead of the museum''s 17:30 last admission**, with room to take in the highlights before the 18:00 close (this is a highlight loop around the gold crown, the Emille Bell and the Wolji Hall, not a sweep of the whole museum). And **in winter that same hour is the night-view hour instead**, so from November to February we head five minutes down the road to Donggung & Wolji — a picture the summer timetable simply cannot produce. Both options are flat walking, which makes them an easy last stop after a long day.","_poi_meta":{"poi_key":"gyeongju_national_museum","kb_version":"1.25","verified_date":"2026-08-04","sources":["Gyeongju National Museum official","Wikipedia","Cultural Heritage Administration of Korea","Yonhap News (2025 APEC special exhibition)","National Museum of Korea (Gyeongju Branch official)"],"seasonal_alternate_poi_key":"donggung_wolji_pond","seasonal_alternate_months":"11,12,1,2","seasonal_alternate_note":"Nov–Feb the last stop becomes Donggung & Wolji (Historic Site No. 18, 09:00–22:00, adult ₩3,000) because sunset falls at 17:15–17:30. Museum hours corrected 2026-08-04: 10:00–18:00, last admission 17:30, closed only Jan 1 / Seollal / Chuseok — NOT Mondays."},"smartNotes":{"photo":"Sacred Bell of King Seongdeok in its outdoor pavilion (afternoon side-light); Cheonmachong gold crown display in Silla Hall (low-light, no flash); the museum garden with stone pagoda fragments","facilities":"Museum shop with replica gold-crown jewelry; café in the main building; cloakroom available","tip":"No flash photography; some special exhibitions prohibit all photography (check signage); allow ~10 minutes for the bell pavilion alone — it is the single most important Silla artifact"},"convenience":{"restroom":"Multiple restrooms in main building + outdoor pavilion area","parking":"Free on-site coach and car parking"},"visitBasics":{"hours":"10:00–18:00 (last admission 17:30) · open until 20:00 on Saturdays from March to October","closed":"Jan 1 · Seollal (Lunar New Year) · Chuseok only — no weekly closing day","admission":"Permanent exhibitions free (paid special exhibitions are separate) · the winter alternate, Donggung & Wolji, is adult ₩3,000","walking":"Mostly indoor + paved outdoor; fully wheelchair-accessible"},"image":"/images/tours/gyeongju-national-museum/chatgpt-image-2026-5-11-12-21-49.webp","images":["/images/tours/gyeongju-national-museum/chatgpt-image-2026-5-11-12-21-49.webp","/images/tours/gyeongju-national-museum/chatgpt-image-2026-5-11-12-21-56.webp","/images/tours/gyeongju-national-museum/chatgpt-image-2026-5-11-12-31-57.webp"],"imageCredits":[{"url":"/images/tours/gyeongju-national-museum/chatgpt-image-2026-5-11-12-21-49.webp","source":"atoc-korea"},{"url":"/images/tours/gyeongju-national-museum/chatgpt-image-2026-5-11-12-21-56.webp","source":"atoc-korea"},{"url":"/images/tours/gyeongju-national-museum/chatgpt-image-2026-5-11-12-31-57.webp","source":"atoc-korea"}],"galleryItems":[{"id":1,"type":"photo","src":"/images/tours/gyeongju-national-museum/chatgpt-image-2026-5-11-12-21-49.webp","location":"Gyeongju National Museum","caption":"Gyeongju National Museum — photo 1","alt":"Gyeongju National Museum — gallery image 1"},{"id":2,"type":"photo","src":"/images/tours/gyeongju-national-museum/chatgpt-image-2026-5-11-12-21-56.webp","location":"Gyeongju National Museum","caption":"Gyeongju National Museum — photo 2","alt":"Gyeongju National Museum — gallery image 2"},{"id":3,"type":"photo","src":"/images/tours/gyeongju-national-museum/chatgpt-image-2026-5-11-12-31-57.webp","location":"Gyeongju National Museum","caption":"Gyeongju National Museum — photo 3","alt":"Gyeongju National Museum — gallery image 3"}]},{"number":8,"time":"≈ 18:05","duration":"75 min","name":"Return drive to Busan","category":"Return — Gyeongbu Expressway","description":"The return drive from Gyeongju to Busan runs on the **Gyeongbu Expressway**, with drop-offs in reverse order: **Haeundae Station → Seomyeon Station → Busan Station**. Around 70–80 minutes depending on traffic. Expected drop-off times are **Haeundae Station ≈19:15 · Seomyeon Station ≈19:35 · Busan Station ≈19:50**. \n\nThe drop-off points are **the same subway exits you boarded at in the morning** — and Busan Station means the Line 1 subway station Exit 4, not the KTX railway terminal.","highlights":["Reverse-order drop-off: Haeundae Station → Seomyeon Station → Busan Station","Expected: Haeundae ≈19:15 · Seomyeon ≈19:35 · Busan Station ≈19:50","**Drop-off points = the same subway exits you boarded at in the morning**"],"timeUsed":["Gyeongju → Busan drive (≈70–80 min)","Three drop-off points (≈5 min total)"],"whyOnRoute":"Reverse-order drop-off is the most time-efficient pattern, and because guests step off exactly where they stepped on in the morning, nobody has to work out a new way home.","_poi_meta":{"poi_key":"OPS_busan_return","kb_version":"1.16","verified_date":"2026-04-29"},"_role":"dropoff"}],"routeFlowStops":[{"name":"Busan subway pickup (3 stations)","type":"origin","theme":"Departure"},{"name":"Ahopsan Bamboo Forest (or Haeundae Dalmaji Hill in cherry season)","type":"primary","theme":"Forest / Coastal road (seasonal)"},{"name":"Bulguksa Temple (UNESCO)","type":"primary","theme":"UNESCO heritage"},{"name":"Lunch (Gyeongju ssambap)","type":"meal","theme":"Meal"},{"name":"Gyochon Hanok Village & Choi Family House & Woljeonggyo Bridge","type":"primary","theme":"Head-family hanok + Silla bridge"},{"name":"Daereungwon Tomb Complex & Hwangnidan-gil","type":"primary","theme":"Royal tombs + cafe street"},{"name":"Gyeongju National Museum (winter: Donggung & Wolji by night)","type":"primary","theme":"Silla artifacts / winter night view"},{"name":"Busan subway drop-off","type":"return","theme":"Return"}],"routePhases":[{"phase":"Outbound + Ahopsan","duration":"≈ 2.5 hours","description":"Pickup at three Busan subway stations (Busan Station Exit 4 at 08:10, Seomyeon Station Exit 4 at 08:30, Haeundae Station Exit 5 at 09:10), then ≈30 min to Ahopsan Bamboo Forest in Gijang and a 60-minute walk along the bamboo paths."},{"phase":"UNESCO core","duration":"≈ 2 hours","description":"Drive ≈50 min from Ahopsan to Bulguksa, then 75 minutes walking the temple at a relaxed pace."},{"phase":"Lunch","duration":"≈ 1 hour","description":"Regional food such as ssambap at a local restaurant in central Gyeongju (≈55 min, paid individually)."},{"phase":"Gyeongju walking block 1 — head family and bridge","duration":"≈ 1.5 hours","description":"From Gyochon Hanok Village and the Choi Family House along the Namcheon riverside to Woljeonggyo Bridge — 75 minutes on a single drop-off."},{"phase":"Gyeongju walking block 2 — royal tombs and cafe street","duration":"≈ 1.5 hours","description":"A walk among the Daereungwon mounds, then over the west wall into Hwangnidan-gil — 75 minutes on a single drop-off."},{"phase":"Final stop + return","duration":"≈ 2.5 hours","description":"60 minutes at Gyeongju National Museum (Nov–Feb: Donggung & Wolji by night), then back to Busan on the Gyeongbu Expressway — Haeundae ≈19:15, Seomyeon ≈19:35, Busan Station ≈19:50."}],"routeShapeIntro":{"title":"How this day flows","subtitle":"A year-round day trip that starts in Busan and walks Gyeongju in a single day. The morning is two places reached by road (Ahopsan Bamboo Forest and Bulguksa); the afternoon is two blocks walked with the van parked — from Gyochon Hanok Village and the Choi Family House across the Namcheon to Woljeonggyo, and from the Daereungwon tombs over one wall into Hwangnidan-gil. The last slot belongs to the season: March to October it is Gyeongju National Museum, where the actual crown from the tomb you just walked through is on display, and November to February that same hour is the night-view hour, so it becomes Donggung & Wolji. Built for travellers who care less about how many sights they ticked off than about how long they got to stay at each one."},"whyTourWorks":{"bestFor":["Travelers who want Gyeongju''s classic stops without rushing","First-time visitors to Korea","Adult families and couples","Travelers who like an afternoon that is walked, not driven — Choi Family House to Woljeonggyo, Daereungwon to Hwangnidan-gil","On winter departures, travelers who want Gyeongju''s best night view (Donggung & Wolji) built into the itinerary"],"lessIdealFor":["Travelers who need an early evening return — Busan drop-offs run ≈19:15–19:50","Travelers needing minimal walking (≈5–5.5 km across the day)","Stroller-heavy groups (Bulguksa has stone steps — though Daereungwon is flat)","Travelers needing a tight same-day flight buffer"],"routeLogicSections":[{"title":"Why this route is built this way","icon":"route","iconBg":"bg-sky-50/80","iconColor":"text-sky-600","items":[{"label":"The afternoon is bundled into two blocks walked with the van parked","detail":"Gyochon Hanok Village and the Choi Family House are a 10–15 minute riverside walk from Woljeonggyo along the Namcheon, and Daereungwon and Hwangnidan-gil are separated by a single wall. So instead of four stops for four places we make only two drop-offs — the time that used to go into boarding and unboarding becomes walking time, and each block gets 75 minutes."},{"label":"The season decides the last slot — museum or night view","detail":"From March to October we reach Gyeongju National Museum at about 17:00 (30 minutes ahead of its 17:30 last admission). From November to February the sun is already going down at that same hour, so we drive five minutes to Donggung & Wolji instead and watch it from dusk through to the moment the lights come on. It is a picture the summer timetable cannot make."},{"label":"See it at the tomb, then see it again at the museum","detail":"You stand at the 1973 excavation site inside Cheonmachong at Daereungwon, and then meet the actual Cheonmachong Gold Crown (National Treasure No. 188) from that tomb at the next stop, Gyeongju National Museum. The same goes for the 33,000-plus artifacts in the Wolji Hall — they came out of the bed of the pond at Donggung & Wolji, the winter alternate. The order itself does the explaining."},{"label":"Every pickup point is a subway station exit","detail":"Busan Station Exit 4 (Line 1), Seomyeon Station Exit 4 (Lines 1 & 2), Haeundae Station Exit 5 (Line 2). At Busan Station in particular, we meet at Exit 4 of the subway station in front of the building, not inside the KTX railway terminal — the train station plaza mixes buses and taxis and makes a vehicle hard to find, while a subway exit number is something anyone finds in one go. Drop-off is at the same three points."},{"label":"Mini-van size, between a coach and a private car","detail":"Coach tours from Busan carry 35–45 passengers cheaply; private tours carry 1–4 at a much higher rate. This small-group mini-van sits between the two — fewer people means faster reassembly at each stop, and that time turns straight back into time on site."}]}]},"practicalAccordionItems":[{"id":"inclusions","title":"What''s included","preview":"Round-trip mini-van transport from Busan, certified English/Chinese-speaking guide,…","content":["Round-trip mini-van transport from Busan, certified English/Chinese-speaking guide, fuel/tolls/parking.","Not included: lunch (≈₩15,000–₩25,000), Ahopsan Bamboo Forest admission (₩5,000, paid on site), Cheonmachong interior (adult ₩3,000, optional), Donggung & Wolji admission on winter departures (adult ₩3,000), tips.","All other site admissions are free: Bulguksa (free since May 2023), Gyeongju National Museum permanent exhibitions (free since 2008), Gyochon Hanok Village and the Choi Family House, Woljeonggyo Bridge, the Daereungwon park grounds, Hwangnidan-gil."]},{"id":"pickup","title":"Pickup & drop-off","preview":"Pickup runs in sequence at three subway stations in central Busan: Busan Station Exit 4…","content":["Pickup runs in sequence at three subway stations in central Busan: Busan Station Exit 4 (Line 1) at 08:10, Seomyeon Station Exit 4 (Lines 1 & 2) at 08:30, Haeundae Station Exit 5 (Line 2) at 09:10.","Busan Station means Exit 4 of the Line 1 subway station, not the KTX railway terminal. If you wait in the train station concourse or on the plaza you will not see the vehicle.","Drop-off is in reverse order, at the same exits you boarded at: Haeundae Station ≈19:15, Seomyeon Station ≈19:35, Busan Station ≈19:50.","Provide a WhatsApp or KakaoTalk number at booking; the guide will message vehicle details the day before."]},{"id":"walking","title":"Walking & terrain","preview":"Moderate.","content":["Moderate.","Total walking distance across the day is ≈5–5.5 km — more than the previous course, because the two afternoon blocks (Gyochon ↔ Woljeonggyo, Daereungwon ↔ Hwangnidan-gil) are linked on foot.","Bulguksa has stone steps between terraces. Gyochon, Woljeonggyo, Daereungwon and Hwangnidan-gil are all flat, and Daereungwon''s grass paths are comfortable for strollers and older guests.","Climbing the tomb mounds is prohibited (fines apply). Comfortable closed-toe shoes essential — Bulguksa stone is uneven."]},{"id":"cancellation","title":"Cancellation","preview":"Free cancellation up to 24 hours before pickup.","content":["Free cancellation up to 24 hours before pickup.","Same-day cancellations and no-shows are non-refundable.","If the tour is cancelled because of force majeure, severe weather, or road closure, full refund applies."]},{"id":"seasonal","title":"Seasonal route notes","preview":"Cherry blossom season (mid-March to mid-April; exact dates vary year-to-year): Ahopsan…","content":["Cherry blossom season (mid-March to mid-April; exact dates vary year-to-year): Ahopsan Bamboo Forest is replaced by Haeundae Dalmaji Hill, Busan''s coastal cherry-tunnel road. The cherry blossoms on Daereungwon''s stone-wall lane peak in early April.","Winter (November–February): because sunset falls at 17:15–17:30, the last stop is swapped from Gyeongju National Museum to the Donggung & Wolji night view (admission adult ₩3,000, paid on site). It is the hour that runs from dusk to the lights coming on.","In summer (June–August) sunset is around 19:30, so a night-view stop does not work and the last stop stays fixed as Gyeongju National Museum."]}],"practicalWeatherStatic":{"today":{"temp":"—","label":"See seasonal notes"},"tomorrow":{"temp":"—","label":"See seasonal notes"},"_seasonalSummary":"Gyeongju is inland southeast Korea — winters slightly colder than coastal Busan, summers hotter. Spring and autumn are the most comfortable seasons."},"seasonalVariations":[{"season":"Spring (mid-Mar to mid-Apr, cherry blossom season)","description":"The Ahopsan opener swaps for the Haeundae Dalmaji Hill cherry-blossom road. Daereungwon''s stone-wall lane is one of Gyeongju''s finest cherry tunnels (with a festival in early April every year), and Gyochon village blossoms in early April too. The last stop is Gyeongju National Museum."},{"season":"Summer (Jun–Aug)","description":"The bamboo forest stays cool and the museum is air-conditioned. Both afternoon walking blocks have shade and cafes to rest in. Sunset is around 19:30, so a night view does not fit the route and the last stop stays Gyeongju National Museum."},{"season":"Autumn (late Sep to late Oct)","description":"Gyochon''s ginkgos peak in late October, and the grass mounds of Daereungwon make the easiest walking of the day. The last stop is Gyeongju National Museum, which from March to October stays open until 20:00 on Saturdays."},{"season":"Winter (Nov–Feb)","description":"The quietest season of the year. Because sunset falls at 17:15–17:30, the last stop is swapped from Gyeongju National Museum to the Donggung & Wolji night view — you watch it from dusk through to the lights coming on, and this is the only season it is possible. Woljeonggyo also catches the sunset light on winter afternoons."}],"bookingTrustItems":[{"icon":"check-circle","title":"Licensed local tour provider","description":"Authorized Korean tour provider with insurance","iconBg":"bg-emerald-50/80","iconColor":"text-emerald-600","body":"Licensed local tour provider running an unhurried Gyeongju circuit, with drop-off back in Busan around 19:50."},{"icon":"route","title":"Route specialists","description":"Hand-tuned itinerary, not a generic loop","iconBg":"bg-sky-50/80","iconColor":"text-sky-600","body":"Route specialists who have refined the Busan-Gyeongju loop over hundreds of runs — Ahopsan bamboo forest opens the day before midday tour-bus crowds arrive."},{"icon":"users","title":"Comfortable group size","description":"Right-sized for the route","iconBg":"bg-amber-50/80","iconColor":"text-amber-600","body":"Small-group mini-van (max ~10) keeps Bulguksa''s six National Treasures and the National Museum galleries comfortable and unhurried."}],"bookingSupportSteps":[{"timing":"Immediately","title":"Instant Confirmation","detail":"Booking confirmation with itinerary summary","description":"Instant confirmation with pickup station choice (Busan Station / Seomyeon / Haeundae) and the day''s schedule."},{"timing":"12 hours before","title":"12-Hour Reminder","detail":"Weather update and any route adjustments","description":"Seasonal note (cherry blossom alt route in mid-March to mid-April; winter bridge lighting period) sent the day before."},{"timing":"Evening before","title":"Final Pickup Info","detail":"Exact pickup time and driver contact","description":"Driver and van details confirmed the evening before departure."},{"timing":"Morning of tour","title":"Day-of Route Notes","detail":"Morning briefing based on live conditions","description":"Morning pickup confirmation message sent to your phone."},{"timing":"During tour","title":"Stop-by-Stop Tips","detail":"Real-time guidance at each location","description":"Guide provides full narration — bamboo forest clan history, Silla-era temple art, National Museum gold crown, Choi family ethics, Woljeonggyo''s 760 CE origins."},{"timing":"After tour","title":"Post-tour Support","detail":"Follow-up and recommendations","description":"Post-tour feedback request sent after drop-off."}],"staticQuestions":[{"id":"q-vs-unesco-legacy","question":"How does the day flow?","answer":"The morning is two places reached by road; the afternoon is two blocks covered on foot. You leave the three Busan subway stations between 08:10 and 09:10, then Ahopsan Bamboo Forest (60 min) → Bulguksa (75 min) → lunch in central Gyeongju (60 min) closes out the morning. In the afternoon the van stops only twice — from Gyochon Hanok Village and the Choi Family House across the Namcheon to Woljeonggyo (75 min), and from the Daereungwon tombs over one wall into Hwangnidan-gil (75 min). The last slot belongs to the season: March to October it is Gyeongju National Museum (arriving ≈17:00, ahead of its 17:30 last admission), and November to February that same hour is the night-view hour, so it becomes Donggung & Wolji. Drop-off in Busan is Haeundae Station ≈19:15, Seomyeon Station ≈19:35 and Busan Station ≈19:50 — ≈11.5 hours in total."},{"id":"q-bridge-night-view","question":"Will we see Woljeonggyo Bridge illuminated at night?","answer":"On this tour you see Woljeonggyo in daylight, because we arrive around 2 pm together with Gyochon Hanok Village and the Choi Family House — and the timber joinery detail and the reflections on the river are actually clearer by day, with none of the sunset crowd. If you want the illumination, book a winter departure (Nov–Feb): in that season the last stop becomes the Donggung & Wolji night view, so you see Gyeongju''s most famous night scene right at the hour the lights come on."},{"id":"q-bulguksa-only","question":"Why no Seokguram Grotto?","answer":"Seokguram is on the same UNESCO inscription as Bulguksa but sits 8 km up the mountain by a steep narrow road, with parking constraints and a 10-minute walk from the lot. The combination makes it impractical to add to a day that also includes the museum, Gyochon, and the bridge. Most one-day Gyeongju tours include Bulguksa or Seokguram, not both."},{"id":"q-admissions","question":"Are admissions included?","answer":"Most are free. Free: Bulguksa (since May 2023), the Gyeongju National Museum permanent exhibition (since 2008), Gyochon Hanok Village and the Choi Family House, Woljeonggyo Bridge, the Daereungwon park grounds, and Hwangnidan-gil. \n\nPaid on site: Ahopsan Bamboo Forest ₩5,000 (required) · the Cheonmachong tomb interior ₩3,000 inside Daereungwon (optional — you can walk the mounds without going in) · on winter departures (Nov–Feb) the last stop changes, so Donggung & Wolji ₩3,000 · lunch ₩15,000–₩25,000. \n\nTypical total out-of-pocket per person therefore runs about ₩20,000–₩33,000 in summer and about ₩23,000–₩36,000 in winter."},{"id":"q-museum-language","question":"Is there English signage in Gyeongju National Museum?","answer":"Yes. Major exhibits carry bilingual Korean/English panels, and a recent renovation added digital labels. The permanent exhibitions are free, hours are 10:00–18:00 (last admission 17:30), and regular closures are Jan 1, Seollal and Chuseok only — so it is open as normal on Mondays. This tour arrives around 17:00 and spends 60 minutes on the highlights: the gold crown, the Emille Bell and the Wolji Hall. Winter departures (Nov–Feb) replace the museum with the Donggung & Wolji night view."},{"id":"q-bulguksa-photography","question":"Can I take photos at Bulguksa?","answer":"Yes throughout the temple grounds, including the two pagodas and lower-terrace stone bridges. Avoid flash and tripods inside the worship halls where services may be in progress. Cherry blossoms peak in mid-April; autumn foliage in late October — the most-photographed times."},{"id":"q-vegetarian","question":"Are there vegetarian options for lunch?","answer":"Yes. Gyeongju ssambap is naturally vegetable-heavy; ordering it without meat banchan is straightforward. The guide can also route to a temple-cuisine (사찰음식) restaurant for fully vegetarian meals on request — please advise at booking."},{"id":"q-rain","question":"What if it rains?","answer":"Light rain: the tour runs as planned. \n\nHeavy rain: Bulguksa''s stone steps get slippery and Ahopsan''s earth paths get muddy, so the guide trims outdoor dwell and shifts the balance indoors. From March to October that means more time at Gyeongju National Museum — still the last stop, indoors and climate-controlled — and at Daereungwon less time among the mounds in exchange for more time in Hwangnidan-gil''s indoor cafes and shops. In winter (Nov–Feb) the last stop is the open-air Donggung & Wolji pond, so in rain it can be swapped back to the museum, provided arrival still clears the 17:30 last admission. \n\nTyphoon-grade weather: rescheduling or a full refund."},{"id":"first-time","question":"Is this a good choice for first-time visitors to Gyeongju?","answer":"Yes. The day covers Gyeongju''s headline sites at an unhurried pace, which suits a first visit well. What makes the difference is that the two afternoon blocks are walked — from the Choi Family House across to Woljeonggyo, and from the Daereungwon mounds over one wall into Hwangnidan-gil — so you come away having seen the texture of the city, not just a checklist of monuments. \n\nThat said, if you want an early evening return, this is not the tour for you: the day runs ≈11.5 hours and Busan drop-offs are 19:15–19:50. In that case a shorter Busan-area tour is the better choice."},{"id":"walking","question":"How much walking is there?","answer":"≈5–5.5 km across the day. It grew from the previous version of this route because both afternoon blocks are now linked on foot — Gyochon to Woljeonggyo along the Namcheon (10–15 min), and Daereungwon out through its west gate into Hwangnidan-gil. Bulguksa is the most active part (uneven stone, gentle uphill); Daereungwon''s grass paths are flat and step-free; the museum is flat indoor walking. Comfortable closed-toe shoes essential."},{"id":"family","question":"Is this suitable for families?","answer":"Yes for school-age children (≈8+) who can manage a full-day rhythm. The museum''s gold crown and the giant bell are typically the kid-favorite stops; the bamboo forest is sensory-friendly. Younger children may flag during the museum visit."},{"id":"seniors","question":"Can older adults join comfortably?","answer":"Recommended for active seniors comfortable with ≈5–5.5 km of walking and uneven stone at Bulguksa. The afternoon is easier than the distance suggests — Daereungwon is flat, step-free lawn, and Hwangnidan-gil has plenty of cafes to sit down in. Do be aware the day is long: ≈11.5 hours, with drop-off back in Busan around 19:50."},{"id":"children","question":"What''s the recommended minimum age?","answer":"Best fit is around age 8 and up. The day covers ≈5–5.5 km of walking and runs ≈11.5 hours (08:10 first pickup, ≈19:50 last drop), so a child needs to be comfortable with that rhythm. The two afternoon blocks are actually the kid-friendliest part of the day — Daereungwon is flat, step-free lawn paths with room to move, and Hwangnidan-gil has cafes and Hwangnam bread. The museum''s quiet galleries and the early start are the harder parts for young children."},{"id":"q-recourse-1","question":"What do we do on Hwangnidan-gil?","answer":"It is a street of cafes, studios and gift shops converted out of hanok houses, directly across Daereungwon''s west wall. The name means ''Hwangnam-dong''s Gyeongnidan-gil'', and since around 2017 it has been the youngest street in Gyeongju. You get roughly 20–30 minutes of free time — enough for a coffee, a box of Hwangnam bread (the red-bean pastry that started in this neighbourhood in 1939), and a restroom stop. Anything you buy is at your own expense."},{"id":"q-recourse-2","question":"Do I miss Gyeongju National Museum if I go in winter?","answer":"Yes — on Nov–Feb departures the last stop is replaced by the Donggung & Wolji night view. In winter the sun is already low by around 5 pm, which shortens a museum visit, while that same hour is exactly when Gyeongju''s best night view begins. The two sites are 5 minutes apart by car, and since the 33,000-plus artifacts in the museum''s Wolji Hall came out of the bed of that very pond, the story still connects. If the museum is essential for you, choose a departure between March and October."},{"id":"q-recourse-3","question":"Is the pickup point a train station or a subway station?","answer":"All three are subway station exits — Busan Station Exit 4 (Subway Line 1), Seomyeon Station Exit 4 (Lines 1 & 2), Haeundae Station Exit 5 (Line 2). At Busan Station in particular we meet at Exit 4 of the subway station in front of the building, not inside the KTX railway terminal. If you wait in the train station concourse or on the plaza you will not see the vehicle, so please navigate by the subway exit number. Drop-off is at the same three points."}],"guestReviews":[],"reviewsSummary":{"averageRating":0,"totalReviews":0,"ratingDistribution":[{"stars":5,"count":0,"percentage":0},{"stars":4,"count":0,"percentage":0},{"stars":3,"count":0,"percentage":0},{"stars":2,"count":0,"percentage":0},{"stars":1,"count":0,"percentage":0}],"highlights":[]},"sticky_booking_bar":{"note":"Select your date and group size to check live availability and complete your booking.","price":{"amountLabel":"39","currency":"USD","per":"person","salePriceUsd":39,"originalPriceUsd":50,"discountPercent":22}},"pickup_dropoff":{"departure":[{"lat":35.11449,"lng":129.03933,"name":"Busan Station Exit 4 (Subway Line 1 — NOT the KTX railway station)","type":"station","order":1,"time":"08:10"},{"lat":35.15782,"lng":129.06003,"name":"Seomyeon Station Exit 4 (Subway Lines 1 & 2)","type":"station","order":2,"time":"08:30"},{"lat":35.16396,"lng":129.15853,"name":"Haeundae Station Exit 5 (Subway Line 2)","type":"station","order":3,"time":"09:10"}],"return":[{"lat":35.16396,"lng":129.15853,"name":"Haeundae Station Exit 5 (Subway Line 2)","type":"station","order":1},{"lat":35.15782,"lng":129.06003,"name":"Seomyeon Station Exit 4 (Subway Lines 1 & 2)","type":"station","order":2},{"lat":35.11449,"lng":129.03933,"name":"Busan Station Exit 4 (Subway Line 1 — NOT the KTX railway station)","type":"station","order":3}],"notes":["All three pickup and drop-off points are subway station exits — Busan Station Exit 4 (Line 1), Seomyeon Station Exit 4 (Lines 1 & 2), Haeundae Station Exit 5 (Line 2). At Busan Station we meet at the subway exit in front of the building, NOT inside the KTX railway terminal. Choose the exit closest to your hotel when you book. The tour runs year-round; in cherry blossom season (mid-March to mid-April) Ahopsan Bamboo Forest is replaced by Haeundae Dalmaji Hill, and in winter (Nov–Feb) the last stop changes from Gyeongju National Museum to the Donggung & Wolji night view."]},"matching_profile":{"profile_version":6,"is_active":true,"product_id":"from-busan-gyeongju-ancient-capital-day-tour","product_type":"small_group","route_type":"fixed_route","destination_region":"busan_gyeongju","region_type":"gyeongju_from_busan","vehicle_type":"minicoach","duration_hours":11.5,"region_tags":["busan_central","gyeongju_central"],"theme_tags":["unesco","history","ancient_capital","storytelling_focus","calm_pace","shorter_day"],"poi_tags":["ahopsan_bamboo_forest","bulguksa_temple","gyochon_hanok_village","woljeonggyo_bridge","daereungwon_tomb_complex","hwangnidan_gil","gyeongju_national_museum","donggung_wolji_pond"],"pace_level":0.4,"walking_level":0.7,"scenic_level":0.8,"photo_level":0.8,"culture_level":1,"relax_level":0.8,"first_time_fit":1,"family_fit":0.6,"senior_fit":0.6,"couple_fit":0.95,"solo_traveler_fit":0.85,"active_traveler_fit":0.6,"adult_family_fit":1,"family_with_teens_fit":1,"young_kids_fit":0.4,"senior_active_fit":0.6,"senior_general_fit":0.6,"mobility_friendly_fit":0.6,"stroller_fit":0.6,"one_day_fit":0.8,"same_day_flight_fit":0.4,"rain_fit":0.65,"value_for_money_fit":1,"iconic_landmark_fit":1,"cafe_fit":0.75,"food_fit":0.6,"nature_fit":0.6,"beach_fit":0.2,"nightlife_fit":0.3,"shopping_fit":0.45,"no_shopping_fit":1,"local_culture_f